ch row we examine all possible pairs among the three remaining points; a pair must lie on a sunny line.\n\n* For \\(x=1\\): the three remaining points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Their pairwise slopes are  \n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &(2,1)-(2,2):\\ \\text{vertical (slope }\\infty),\\\\\n  &(2,1)-(3,1):\\ \\text{horizontal (slope }0),\\\\\n  &(2,2)-(3,1):\\ \\text{slope }-1 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  All are non\u2011sunny, so no sunny line can contain any pair.\n\n* For \\(y=1\\): the remaining points are \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\).  \n  Their slopes are vertical, horizontal, and \\(-1\\) respectively \u2013 again all non\u2011sunny.\n\n* For \\(x+y=4\\): the remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Their slopes are vertical, horizontal, and \\(-1\\) \u2013 none is sunny.\n\nThus a non\u2011sunny line covering three points cannot be part of a valid configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Non\u2011sunny line containing exactly two points  \n\nThe only non\u2011sunny lines that contain exactly two points of \\(T_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\nx=2,\\qquad y=2,\\qquad x+y=3 .\n\\]\n\nWe treat each case.\n\n* **Case \\(x=2\\).**  \n  Covered points: \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \n  Any sunny line can contain at most two of them.  \n  The only pair among the four with a sunny slope is \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n  The two points left, \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\), are vertical, hence cannot lie on a sunny line.  \n  Therefore the four points cannot be split into two sunny pairs.\n\n* **Case \\(y=2\\).**  \n  Covered points: \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n  The only sunny pair among them is \\((1,3)\\) with \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \n  The leftover points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) are horizontal, so no sunny line can contain them together.  \n  Hence impossible.\n\n* **Case \\(x+y=3\\)