  \nIf three points of \\(T_{3}\\) were collinear, their coordinates would satisfy one of the following:\n\n* all have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \u2192 a vertical line, parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis;\n* all have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate \u2192 a horizontal line, parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis;\n* they satisfy \\(x+y=\\text{constant}\\) \u2192 a line of slope \\(-1\\), parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nAll three possibilities are non\u2011sunny. Hence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_{3}\\). \u220e  \n\nConsequently the two sunny lines together can cover at most \\(2+2=4\\) points.  \nSince \\(|T_{3}|=6\\), the remaining (non\u2011sunny) line must cover at least two points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Possible non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nA non\u2011sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=d\\)).  \nAmong the points of \\(T_{3}\\) the only such lines that contain **three** points are  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=4 .\n\\]\n\nAll other non\u2011sunny lines contain at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\).  \nWe treat the two possibilities separately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non\u2011sunny line containing three points  \n\n| non\u2011sunny line | points covered | remaining points |\n|----------------|----------------|------------------|\n| \\(x=1\\)        | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) |\n| \\(y=1\\)        | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\) |\n| \\(x+y=4\\)      | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) |\n\nFor each row we examine the three remaining points.  \nTheir pairwise slopes are respectively\n\n* \\(x=1\\): vertical, horizontal, and \\(-1\\);\n* \\(y=1\\): vertical, horizontal, and \\(-1\\);\n* \\(x+y=4\\): vertical, horizontal, and \\(-1\\).\n\nAll are non\u2011sunny, so no sunny line can contain any pair of the remaining points.  \nWith only two sunny lines available, at most two of the three points could be covered, leaving at least one uncovered.  \nHence a non\u2011sunny line covering three points cannot be part of a valid configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Non\u2011sunny line containing exactly two points  \n\nThe only non\u2011sunny lines that contain exactly two points of \\(T_{3}\\) are  \n\n\\[\nx=2,\\qquad y=2,\\qquad x+y=3 .\n\\]\n\nWe treat each case.\n\n* **Case \\(x=2\\).**  \n  Covered points: \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \n\n  Slopes between the remaining points are  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &(1,1)-(1,2):\\ \\infty\\ (\\text{vertical}),\\\\\n  &(1,1)-(1,3):\\ \\infty,\\\\\n  &(1,1)-(3,1):\\ 0\\ (\\text{horizontal}),\\\\\n  &(1,2)-(1,3):\\ \\infty,\\\\\n  &(1,2)-(3,1):\\ -\\tfrac12\\ (\\text{sunny}),\\\\\n  &(1,3)-(3,1):\\ -1\\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny}).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  The only sunny pair is \\((1,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  The two points left, \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\), are vertical, hence cannot lie on a sunny line.  \n  Thus the four points cannot be covered by two sunny lines.\n\n* **Case \\(y=2\\).**  \n  Covered points: \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n\n  Their slopes are  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &(1,1)-(1,3):\\ \\infty,\\\\\n  &(1,1)-(2,1):\\ 0,\\\\\n  &(1,1)-(3,1):\\ 0,\\\\\n  &(1,3)-(2,1):\\ -2\\ (\\text{sunny}),\\\\\n  &(1,3)-(3,1):\\ -1,\\\\\n  &(2,1)-(3,1):\\ 0.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  The only sunny pair is \\((1,3),(2,1)\\).  \n  The leftover points \\((1,1),(3,1)\\) are horizontal, a non\u2011sunny line.  \n  Hence impossible.\n\n* **Case \\(x+y=3\\).**  \n  Covered points: \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n\n  Their slopes are  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &(1,1)-(1,3):\\ \\infty,\\\\\n  &(1,1)-(2,2):\\ 1\\ (\\text{sunny}),\\\\\n  &(1,1)-(3,1):\\ 0,\\\\\n  &(1,3)-(2,2):\\ -1,\\\\\n  &(1,3)-(3,1):\\ -1,\\\\\n  &(2,2)-(3,1):\\ -1.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  The only sunny pair is \\((1,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  The remaining points \\((1,3),(3,1)\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is non\u2011sunny.  \n  Thus the four points cannot be split into two sunny lines.\n\nIn every possible choice for the non\u2011sunny line that covers exactly two points, the four uncovered points cannot be partitioned into two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nAll admissible possibilities for the non\u2011sunny line lead to a contradiction with the requirement that the two remaining lines be sunny and together cover all points of \\(T_{3}\\). Therefore **no** set of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can satisfy the covering condition when \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]"
