n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines contains at least two sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \n   Then at most \\(n-k\\) of the lines are non\u2011sunny.  \n   Because a non\u2011sunny line can be either a diagonal, a horizontal or a vertical line, the only lines that can cover more than one point of a diagonal are the diagonal lines themselves.  \n\n   If the diagonal line \\(x+y=t_0\\) is **absent** from the family, all points of \\(D_{t_0}\\) (there are \\(t_0-1\\) of them) must be covered by the remaining lines, each of which can contribute at most one point of \\(D_{t_0}\\).  \n   Hence we would need at least \\(t_0-1\\) distinct lines just for that diagonal.  \n\n   For \\(n\\ge 4\\) we have \\(t_0\\ge 3\\), so \\(t_0-1\\ge 2\\).  \n   If two different diagonals were omitted we would need at least\n   \\[\n   (t_0-1)+(t_1-1)\\ge 2+2=4\n   \\]\n   extra lines, which together with the \\(n-2\\) other lines would exceed the allowed total of \\(n\\).  \n   Therefore **at most one** diagonal line may be omitted.  \n\n   Consequently the number of diagonal lines present is at least \\(n-1\\).  \n   Since every diagonal line is non\u2011sunny, we have\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge n-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 1 .\n   \\]\n   Thus for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) **no covering can contain two or more sunny lines**.\n\n4. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   For \\(n=3\\) the set \\(T_3\\) consists of six points:\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n   The previous upper\u2011bound argument fails because the smallest omitted diagonal would be \\(x+y=3\\) (containing only two points), and the total budget of three lines is sufficient to replace it with sunny lines.\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=3\\) (all three lines sunny).*  \n   Pair the six points as\n   \\[\n   \\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\;\n   \\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\;\n   \\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n   Each pair determines a line whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n