m_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n##### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;x+y=t\\qquad (t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\n*Each line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is non\u2011sunny.*  \n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nConsequently the family \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n##### 2.2  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the diagonal lines with \\(t=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\) and add the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) except \\((1,1)\\) (the only point of \\(D_2\\)).  \nSince \\((1,1)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\), the line \\(S\\) covers the missing point.  \n\nThus we have exactly one sunny line and all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\nBoth constructions are completely verified point\u2011by\u2011point as described above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines when \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\n##### 3.1  A geometric lemma  \n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be any line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen for each diagonal \\(D_t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\)) the intersection \\(\\ell\\cap D_t\\) contains **at most one** lattice point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe diagonal \\(D_t\\) is the set of points satisfying the two linear equations  \n\n\\[\nx+y=t,\\qquad x\\ge 1,\\;y\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), the two equations \\(x+y=t\\) and the equation of \\(\\ell\\) are independent; they determine a unique point in the plane.  \nIf that point happens to be integral and lies inside the triangle, it is the only lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) belonging to \\(D_t\\). \u220e\n\nThe same argument shows that a horizontal line (\\(y=c\\)) or a vertical line (\\(x=c\\)) also meets each diagonal in at most one lattice point.\n\n##### 3.2  Consequence for a covering\n\nConsider a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) and let \\(n-k\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nBecause of the lemma, any line that is **not** a diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) can contribute at most one point to each diagonal \\(D_t\\).  \nHence, for a fixed \\(t\\), the only way to cover **more than one** point of \\(D_t\\) is to include the diagonal line \\(x+y=t\\) itself among the members of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).\n\n##### 3.3  Counting diagonals\n\nAssume that the diagonal line \\(x+y=t_0\\) is **absent** from \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).  \nThen all \\(|D_{t_0}|=t_0-1\\) points of that diagonal must be covered by the remaining lines, each of which can supply at most one point of \\(D_{t_0}\\).  \nConsequently at least \\(t_0-1\\) distinct lines are required solely for the diagonal \\(D_{t_0}\\).\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 4\\) we have \\(t_0\\ge 3\\), so \\(t_0-1\\ge 2\\).  \nIf two different diagonals were omitted, say \\(x+y=t_0\\) and \\(x+y=t_1\\), we would need at least  \n\n\\[\n(t_0-1)+(t_1-1)\\ge 2+2=4\n\\]\n\nadditional lines beyond the \\(n-2\\) lines that could be used for the other diagonals.  \nThat would give a total of at least \\(n+2\\) lines, contradicting the requirement that exactly \\(n\\) lines are used.\n\nTherefore **at most one** diagonal may be missing from the family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).  \nHence the number of diagonal lines present is at least \\(n-1\\).\n\nSince every diagonal line is non\u2011sunny, we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge n-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) a covering by \\(n\\) lines can contain **no more than one** sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  The exceptional case \\(n=3\\)\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\) the set \\(T_3\\) has six points:\n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe argument of Section\u202f3 fails because the smallest possible omitted diagonal is \\(x+y=3\\), which contains only two points; the total budget of three lines is sufficient to replace it with sunny lines.\n\n##### 4.1  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nIf exactly two sunny lines were used, the remaining non\u2011sunny line could be at most one diagonal.  \nRemoving a diagonal leaves at least two points that are not on any diagonal; each of those points would require a distinct sunny line (any line through two of them would be either horizontal, vertical or have slope \\(-1\\), all non\u2011sunny).  \nHence two sunny lines cannot cover all six points; \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n##### 4.2  Construction for \\(k=3\\)\n\nPair the six points as  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\qquad\n\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\qquad\n\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach pair determines a sunny line:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\[2pt]\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\[2pt]\nL_3 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three lines have slopes \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore they are sunny and distinct.\n\n**Verification** (substituting the coordinates):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{Point} & \\text{Line} & \\text{Check}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_1 & 1 = 1 \\\\\n(2,2) & L_1 & 2 = 2 \\\\\n(1,2) & L_2 & 2 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot1 + \\tfrac52 = 2 \\\\\n(3,1) & L_2 & 1 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot3 + \\tfrac52 = 1 \\\\\n(1,3) & L_3 & 3 = -2\\cdot1 + 5 = 3 \\\\\n(2,1) & L_3 & 1 = -2\\cdot2 + 5 = 1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of the three sunny lines, giving a covering with \\(k=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Final classification of admissible \\(k\\)\n\nCombining the constructions of Section\u202f2, the impossibility result of Section\u202f3, and the special analysis of Section\u202f4, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & \\text{if } n\\ge 4,\\\\[4pt]\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & \\text{if } n=3.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThese are **all** non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem."

