\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nD_t=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=t\\},\n\\qquad |D_t|=t-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{t=2}^{n+1}D_t ,\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); equivalently the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n##### 2.1  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0=\\{\\,L_t:x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Each \\(L_t\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), hence non‑sunny.*  \n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of exactly one line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\).  \nThe family consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n##### 2.2  Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_1=\\bigl\\{\\,L_t:x+y=t\\mid t=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{S\\},\n\\qquad S:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal lines \\(L_t\\) (with \\(t\\ge3\\)) cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except the unique point of \\(D_2\\), namely \\((1,1)\\).  \nSince \\((1,1)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\), the sunny line \\(S\\) covers the missing point.  \n\nHence every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_1\\); exactly one line (\\(S\\)) is sunny, so \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  A Lemma on intersections  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen for each diagonal \\(D_t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\)) the set \\(\\ell\\cap D_t\\) contains at most one lattice point.  \n\n*Proof.*  \nThe diagonal \\(D_t\\) is described by the linear equation \\(x+y=t\\) together with the inequalities \\(x\\ge1,\\;y\\ge1\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), its equation together with \\(x+y=t\\) is a system of two independent linear equations, which has a unique solution in the plane.  \nIf that solution happens to be integral and satisfies the inequalities, it is the only lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) lying on \\(D_t\\). ∎  \n\nThe same reasoning shows that a horizontal line (\\(y=c\\)) or a vertical line (\\(x=c\\)) also meets each diagonal in at most one lattice point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines when \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(k\\ge0\\)).  \nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be **non‑sunny**; each of them is either a diagonal, a horizontal, or a vertical line.\n\nBecause of Lemma 1, any non‑diagonal line can contribute **at most one** point to a given diagonal \\(D_t\\).  \nConsequently, if a diagonal \\(D_t\\) is **absent** from \\(\\mathcal L\\), all its \\(|D_t|=t-1\\) points must be supplied by distinct non‑diagonal lines, requiring at least \\(t-1\\) such lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.1  Which diagonals may be missing?\n\nFor \\(t=2\\) we have \\(|D_2|=1\\); the single point \\((1,1)\\) can be covered by any sunny line, so the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) may be omitted without forcing extra lines.\n\nFor any \\(t\\ge3\\) we have \\(t-1\\ge2\\).  \nIf **two** different diagonals with indices \\(t_0,t_1\\ge3\\) were omitted, then at least \\((t_0-1)+(t_1-1)\\ge2+2=4\\) non‑diagonal lines would be needed to cover their points.  \nTogether with the remaining \\(n-2\\) diagonals this would give at least \\((n-2)+4=n+2\\) lines, contradicting the requirement that exactly \\(n\\) lines are used.\n\nHence **at most one** diagonal with index \\(\\ge3\\) can be missing.  \nTherefore the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains **at least \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines**.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.2  Consequence for sunny lines\n\nEvery diagonal line is non‑sunny, so the number of non‑sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\;\\ge\\;n-1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le1 .\n\\]\n\nThus for every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) a covering by \\(n\\) lines can contain **no more than one** sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe treat separately the possibilities \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=3\\).\n\n##### 5.1  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nSuppose a covering uses exactly two sunny lines and one non‑sunny line.  \nThe non‑sunny line must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is either\n\n* a diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(t\\in\\{2,3,4\\}\\);\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\);\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\).\n\nWe examine each possibility.\n\n| Non‑sunny line | Points it covers | Points left uncovered | Can two sunny lines cover them? |\n|----------------|------------------|----------------------|--------------------------------|\n| \\(x+y=2\\) (covers \\((1,1)\\)) | \\((1,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | Any line through two of these points has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); thus a sunny line can contain **at most one** of them. Two sunny lines can cover at most two of the five remaining points, leaving at least three uncovered. |\n| \\(x+y=3\\) (covers \\((1,2),(2,1)\\)) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | The four remaining points are pairwise such that any line joining two of them has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence each sunny line can contain at most one of them, so two sunny lines can cover at most two of the four points – at least two stay uncovered. |\n| \\(x+y=4\\) (covers \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\)) | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) | The three uncovered points are collinear with slope \\(-1\\); any line through two of them is non‑sunny. Thus each sunny line can contain at most one of them, leaving at least one point uncovered. |\n| \\(y=c\\) (horizontal) | all points with that \\(y\\)‑coordinate | the other five points | Any two of the remaining points have either equal \\(x\\) or sum \\(=4\\); any line through two of them is non‑sunny. Hence each sunny line can cover at most one of the five points, leaving at least three uncovered. |\n| \\(x=c\\) (vertical) | all points with that \\(x\\)‑coordinate | the other five points | Same reasoning as the horizontal case. |\n\nIn every subcase at least one point of \\(T_3\\) remains uncovered, contradicting the covering requirement.  \nTherefore **\\(k=2\\) is impossible** for \\(n=3\\).\n\n##### 5.2  Construction for \\(k=3\\)\n\nPair the six points of \\(T_3\\) as  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\qquad\n\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\qquad\n\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach pair determines a line:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes (\\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\)) differ from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the lines are sunny and distinct.\n\n**Verification that every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of them:**\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{Point} & \\text{Line containing it} & \\text{Check}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_1 & 1 = 1 \\\\\n(2,2) & L_1 & 2 = 2 \\\\\n(1,2) & L_2 & 2 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot1 + \\tfrac52 = 2 \\\\\n(3,1) & L_2 & 1 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot3 + \\tfrac52 = 1 \\\\\n(1,3) & L_3 & 3 = -2\\cdot1 + 5 = 3 \\\\\n(2,1) & L_3 & 1 = -2\\cdot2 + 5 = 1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus all six points are covered, and the family consists of exactly three sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.  Final classification  \n\nCombining the constructions of Sections 2 and 5 with the impossibility results of Sections 4 and 5.1, we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n\\ge4,\\\\[4pt]\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThese are precisely the non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem."
