t exactly \\(n\\) lines are used.\n\nHence **at most one** diagonal with index \\(\\ge3\\) can be missing.  \nTherefore the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains **at least \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines**.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.2  Consequence for sunny lines\n\nEvery diagonal line is non\u2011sunny, so the number of non\u2011sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\;\\ge\\;n-1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le1 .\n\\]\n\nThus for every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) a covering by \\(n\\) lines can contain **no more than one** sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe treat separately the possibilities \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=3\\).\n\n##### 5.1  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nSuppose a covering uses exactly two sunny lines and one non\u2011sunny line.  \nThe non\u2011sunny line must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is either\n\n* a diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(t\\in\\{2,3,4\\}\\);\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\);\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\).\n\nWe examine each possibility.\n\n| Non\u2011sunny line | Points it covers | Points left uncovered | Can two sunny lines cover them? |\n|----------------|------------------|----------------------|--------------------------------|\n| \\(x+y=2\\) (covers \\((1,1)\\)) | \\((1,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | Any line through two of these points has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); thus a sunny line can contain **at most one** of them. Two sunny lines can cover at most two of the five remaining points, leaving at least three uncovered. |\n| \\(x+y=3\\) (covers \\((1,2),(2,1)\\)) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | The four remaining points are pairwise such that any line joining two of them has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence each sunny line can contain at most one of them, 