            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nD_t=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=t\\},\n\\qquad |D_t|=t-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{t=2}^{n+1}D_t ,\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n##### 2.1  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0=\\{\\,L_t:x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_t\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies exactly the equation of \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\).  \nThe family consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n##### 2.2  Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_1=\n\\Bigl\\{\\,L_t:x+y=t\\mid t=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\Bigr\\}\\cup\\{S\\},\n\\qquad S:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe diagonals \\(L_t\\) (with \\(t\\ge3\\)) cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except the unique point of \\(D_2\\), namely \\((1,1)\\).  \nSince \\((1,1)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\), the sunny line \\(S\\) covers the missing point.  \n\nThus every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_1\\); exactly one line (\\(S\\)) is sunny, giving \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Lemma on intersections  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen for each diagonal \\(D_t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\)) the set \\(\\ell\\cap D_t\\) contains at most one lattice point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe diagonal \\(D_t\\) is described by the linear equation \\(x+y=t\\) together with the inequalities \\(x\\ge1,\\;y\\ge1\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), the two equations \\(x+y=t\\) and the equation of \\(\\ell\\) are independent; they determine a unique point in the plane.  \nIf that point happens to be integral and satisfies the inequalities, it is the only lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) on \\(D_t\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Upper bound for sunny lines when \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.  \nLet the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines be non‑sunny; they may be diagonals, horizontals or verticals.\n\nBecause a non‑sunny line that is not a diagonal is either horizontal or vertical, the **maximum** number of diagonals that can appear in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is \\(n-2\\).  \nConsequently **at least two diagonals are missing** from the family.  \nDenote the missing diagonals by \\(D_{t_1},D_{t_2}\\) with \\(2\\le t_1<t_2\\le n+1\\).\n\nThe smallest possible pair of missing diagonals is \\(D_2\\) (size \\(1\\)) and \\(D_3\\) (size \\(2\\)).  \nTheir union consists of the three points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nAny two of these three points have either the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, or the same sum \\(a+b\\); therefore the line through the pair has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), i.e. it is **non‑sunny**.  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.\n\nSince we have only two sunny lines, they can cover at most two of the three points of \\(D_2\\cup D_3\\), leaving at least one point uncovered – a contradiction.\n\nThus a covering of \\(T_n\\) with exactly two sunny lines is impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\).  \nTogether with the constructions of Section 2 we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge4 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains six points.\n\n##### 5.1  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nSuppose a covering uses exactly two sunny lines; the remaining line is non‑sunny.  \nA non‑sunny line in the plane can be only a diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) (\\(t=2,3,4\\)), a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c=1,2,3\\)) or a vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(c=1,2,3\\)).  \nIn every case the non‑sunny line covers at most three points of \\(T_3\\), so **at least five points** of \\(T_3\\) must be covered by the two sunny lines.\n\n**Claim.** A sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n*Proof of claim.*  \nTake any three distinct points of \\(T_3\\).  \nBecause the six points occupy only three distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates \\(\\{1,2,3\\}\\) and three distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates \\(\\{1,2,3\\}\\), among any three points there are either two with the same \\(x\\), two with the same \\(y\\), or two whose sums \\(a+b\\) coincide.  \nIn the first case the line through the pair is vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)); in the second case it is horizontal (slope