            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nD_t=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=t\\},\n\\qquad |D_t|=t-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{t=2}^{n+1}D_t ,\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical)\nand \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Two universal constructions  \n\n##### 2.1  \\(k=0\\)  \n\nTake the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0=\\{\\,L_t:x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_t\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); consequently\n\\((a,b)\\) satisfies exactly the equation of \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\).  \nThe family consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n##### 2.2  \\(k=1\\)  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_1=\n\\Bigl\\{\\,L_t:x+y=t\\mid t=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\Bigr\\}\\cup\\{S\\},\n\\qquad S:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe diagonals \\(L_t\\) (with \\(t\\ge3\\)) cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except the\nunique point of \\(D_2\\), namely \\((1,1)\\).  \nSince \\((1,1)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\), the sunny line \\(S\\) covers the missing\npoint.  \n\nThus every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_1\\);\nexactly one line (\\(S\\)) is sunny, giving \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Lemma 1 (intersection bound)  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  Then for each diagonal \\(D_t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le\nn+1\\)) the set \\(\\ell\\cap D_t\\) contains at most one lattice point.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(D_t\\) is described by the linear equation \\(x+y=t\\) together with the\ninequalities \\(x\\ge1,\\;y\\ge1\\).  Because \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\), the two equations \\(x+y=t\\) and the equation of \\(\\ell\\) are\nindependent; they determine a unique point in the plane.  If that point happens\nto be integral and satisfies the inequalities, it is the only lattice point of\n\\(\\ell\\) on \\(D_t\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)  \n\nAssume a covering uses exactly two sunny lines.\nSince the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑sunny, at most \\(n-2\\) diagonals can\nappear; consequently **at least two diagonals are missing**.  \nThe smallest possible missing diagonals are \\(D_2\\) and \\(D_3\\); their union is  \n\n\\[\nD_2\\cup D_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line through two of these three points has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); e.g. the line through\n\\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)).  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one**\npoint of the set \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\nWith only two sunny lines we can therefore cover at most two of the three\npoints, leaving at least one uncovered – a contradiction.  \nThus **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\)  \n\nSuppose a covering uses \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines.\nThen at most \\(n-k\\le n-4\\) non‑sunny lines are available, so at least four\ndiagonals are absent.  By discarding the smallest possible diagonals we may\nassume that the missing ones are  \n\n\\[\nD_2,\\;D_3,\\;D_4,\\;D_5 .\n\\]\n\nTheir union consists of the ten points  \n\n\\[\nS:=D_2\\cup D_3\\cup D_4\\cup D_5=\n\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.** *A sunny line contains at most two points of the set \\(S\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any three distinct points of \\(S\\).  Because the \\(x\\)-coordinates belong to\n\\(\\{1,2,3,4\\}\\) and the \\(y\\)-coordinates belong to the same set, among three\npoints there is a pair with equal \\(x\\), equal \\(y\\) or equal sum \\(a+b\\).  The\nline through such a pair has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); all three are\nnon‑sunny.  Consequently a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S\\); it\ncan contain at most two. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe ten points of \\(S\\) must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nBy Lemma 2 each sunny line contributes at most two points, so at most \\(2k\\)\npoints of \\(S\\) can be covered.  For \\(k=4\\) we have \\(2k=8<10\\), a\ncontradiction.  Hence **\\(k=4\\) is impossible**.\n\nIf \\(k\\ge5\\) we would have to omit at least \\(k-3\\) diagonals (otherwise the\nnumber of non‑sunny lines would be \\(>n-k\\)).  Besides the ten points of \\(S\\)\nthe omitted diagonals contribute at least \\((k-3)\\cdot5\\) further points\n(because the smallest diagonal not already missing has size \\(5\\)).  Thus at\nleast  \n\n\\[\n10+5(k-3)=5k-5\n\\]\n\npoints would have to be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines, but Lemma 2 limits the\ntotal number of points they can cover to \\(2k\\).  Since \\(2k<5k-5\\) for every\n\\(k\\ge5\\), a contradiction arises.  Therefore **no covering exists with\n\\(k\\ge4\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.  Construction for \\(k=3\\) (valid for every \\(n\\ge3\\))\n\nOmit the three smallest diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,D_4\\) and keep all the remaining\ndiagonals \\(D_5,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) (there are \\(n-3\\) of them).  \nAdd the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\[2pt]\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\[2pt]\nL_3 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\) are none of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore all\nthree are sunny and distinct.\n\n**Verification that the three lines cover the omitted diagonals.**  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{Point}&\\text{Line}&\\text{Check}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_1 & 1 = 1\\\\\n(2,2) & L_1 & 2 = 2\\\\\n(1,2) & L_2 & 2 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot1 + \\tfrac52 = 2\\\\\n(3,1) & L_2 & 1 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot3 + \\tfrac52 = 1\\\\\n(1,3) & L_3 & 3 = -2\\cdot1 + 5 = 3\\\\\n(2,1) & L_3 & 1 = -2\\cdot2 + 5 = 1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus every point of \\(D_2\\cup D_3\\cup D_4\\) lies on one of the three sunny\nlines.  All points belonging to the retained diagonals lie on their own diagonal,\nso every element of \\(T_n\\) is covered.  Exactly three of the \\(n\\) lines are\nsunny, giving a covering with \\(k=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 7.  Final classification  \n\n*Existence* – constructions for \\(k=0\\) (Section 2.1), \\(k=1\\) (Section 2.2)\nand \\(k=3\\) (Section 6) work for **every** integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n*Non‑existence* – Sections 4 and 5 prove that \\(k=2\\) and every \\(k\\ge4\\) are\nimpossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nConsequently the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
        }
